<PolicyLayout
  title="Student & Family Team Agreement"
  description="The shared expectations for students and families participating in STEM+C robotics programs — Tie Dye Samurai (FRC) and Tie Dye Jedi (FTC)."
  effective="the 2026–2027 season"
>
  <p>
    This agreement replaces the separate Student Code of Conduct, Student Contract, and Parent
    Contract used in prior seasons. It applies to every STEM+C robotics program — both
    <a href="https://tiedyesamurai.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tie Dye Samurai</a> (FIRST
    Robotics Competition) and
    <a href="https://tiedyejedi.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tie Dye Jedi</a> (FIRST Tech
    Challenge).
  </p>
  <p>
    Students and a parent or guardian accept this agreement together during team registration.
    Please read it together rather than separately — most of it is about how we treat each other,
    and that only works if everyone knows what was promised.
  </p>

  <h2>1. How we work together</h2>
  <p>
    We are a team. We do not cast blame, point fingers, or disrespect one another. When something
    goes wrong — and on a robotics team something always goes wrong — we help each other find a
    solution, help each other carry it out, and try to enjoy the process while we do.
  </p>
  <p>
    No engineering problem has only one right answer. A successful team considers everyone's ideas
    and then commits to whatever the team chooses, including when it wasn't your idea.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Treat teammates, mentors, volunteers, and other teams with respect.</li>
    <li>Give your best effort in every role, including the ones that aren't glamorous.</li>
    <li>
      Contribute to all parts of the work, not only the parts you enjoy most. Every member has a
      role that matters, and the person who isn't driving the robot at competition is no less
      essential than the person who is.
    </li>
    <li>
      Show other teams good sportsmanship. Every team that brings a solution to a competition has
      accomplished something.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Attendance and communication</h2>
  <p>
    Robotics is a team activity, so consistent attendance matters more here than in most
    after-school programs — when a member is missing, their part of the robot doesn't happen.
  </p>
  <p>
    We know students have school, family, jobs, and other commitments, and we would much rather
    hear about a conflict early than discover it on a build night. Communicating a conflict is
    always the right move and is never held against anyone.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Attend scheduled meetings, and notify a coach <em>in advance</em> when you cannot.</li>
    <li>
      Competitions are long, tiring, and occasionally frustrating. Members commit to being at
      competition because the team depends on it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Repeatedly missing meetings without communication may affect a student's ability to compete or
      to remain on the team.
    </li>
    <li>
      Students and parents are both responsible for monitoring team communications and responding
      when asked. Team messaging channels are for team business.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Responsibility for your work</h2>
  <p>
    Members take responsibility for finishing assigned work on time. Coaches allow reasonable time
    for every assignment, but a task left undone can block the whole team from moving forward.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you are stuck or falling behind, reach out to a teammate or coach. Asking for help early is
    part of the job; going quiet is the only real failure. If work is repeatedly left unfinished, a
    coach will talk with the student and, as needed, the parent.
  </p>

  <h2>4. The work is the students'</h2>
  <p>
    Our program is <strong>student-led and mentor-guided</strong>. Students make the decisions, solve
    the problems, and do the work. Mentors provide instruction, experience, and supervision.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything the team produces — the robot, code, portfolio, presentations, video, signs, and
    outreach materials — must be created by students, not by adults. Parents and mentors agree not
    to interfere with the team's solutions, however tempting it is to just fix it yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>5. The shop, tools, and safety</h2>
  <p>
    Our shop is located at a private residence. That access is a privilege the team depends on, and
    it asks something of everyone who uses it: respect the property, the neighbors, the equipment,
    and all designated work and parking areas.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Wear closed-toe shoes and clothing appropriate for a workshop. Safety glasses and other
      protective equipment are required wherever posted or directed.
    </li>
    <li>
      Complete the required safety training before using any tool or piece of equipment
      independently.
    </li>
    <li>Take care of tools, equipment, and the workspace, and clean up after yourself.</li>
    <li>
      Food and drink are off limits unless they have been explicitly offered. Meetings are not
      social visits — students should arrive ready to participate and be dropped off and picked up
      promptly.
    </li>
    <li>Parents are always welcome at any meeting.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>6. Dues and financial commitment</h2>
  <p>
    Operating a competitive robotics team costs far more than family dues cover; the remainder comes
    from sponsorships, grants, fundraising, and contributions from mentors and community supporters.
    Current dues, payment schedules, and the payment portal for each team are published on that
    team's site.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>
      No student is turned away over money. Payment plans and financial assistance are available.
    </strong>
    If dues are a hardship, or if circumstances change mid-season, contact us privately at
    <a href="mailto:info@stemplusc.org">info@stemplusc.org</a>. We never want finances — or silence
    about finances — to be the reason a student misses out.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Family participation</h2>
  <p>
    STEM+C is volunteer-run. Parents and guardians agree to support the program's operation, which
    may include volunteering during build season and at competitions.
  </p>
  <p>
    You do not need an engineering background to help meaningfully. Event staffing, travel and meal
    logistics, photography, fundraising, sponsor introductions, and shop organization all keep the
    team running. Parents also agree to discuss this agreement with their student and to
    participate in conversations with coaches about any behavior concerns.
  </p>

  <h2>8. If expectations aren't met</h2>
  <p>
    Students who do not meet these expectations after reasonable warning may be required to be
    supervised by an adult at all times, may lose the ability to compete, or may be asked to leave
    the team. We treat this as a last resort and will always talk with the student and family first.
  </p>

  <h2>Acceptance</h2>
  <p>
    Both a student and a parent or guardian accept this agreement during team registration. Keep a
    copy for your records — this page is the current version, and the effective date above tells you
    which season it governs.
  </p>
  <p>
    This agreement covers conduct and expectations. It is separate from the
    <a href="/policies/consent-release">STEM+C Consent, Assumption of Risk, Release &amp; Emergency
    Authorization</a>, which every participating family also signs, and from any consent forms
    <em>FIRST</em> requires directly through its own registration system.
  </p>
</PolicyLayout>
